\details{
The statements are consequences of the graph as encoded. The graph reads
every pair of its constructs that no proposition joins as having no direct
effect on each other and no unexplained association, so the statements are
the theory's commitments only if its authors meant those omissions. A
construct marked \code{x_latent: true} is read like any other, so a
statement can hold fixed a construct that a test would need a measure of,
and no other set of observed constructs is sought for it.

\code{given = "minimal"} then drops each construct from the conditioning
set, in file order, whenever the rest still separate the pair, which leaves
a minimal separating set: no construct in it can be dropped. Every set tried
lies among the ancestors of the pair, where m-separation is separation in an
undirected graph that holding more constructs fixed cannot reopen
(Richardson, 2003), so one pass suffices. Fewer conditioning constructs mean
fewer measured with error, which matters for the reason given below. The
minimal set returned is one of possibly several, and the file order of the
constructs decides which. Minimal statements suit tests of single pairs.
They are no longer a basis set: unless the distribution satisfies
composition, they do not jointly imply the graph's other independencies,
and their tests are not independent, so they should not be combined into
one test of the whole model, as Shipley's (2000) C statistic combines the
basis set's.

A construct that no proposition relates to another is left out, because
silence about a construct is not a claim that it is independent of anything,
and so is a construct without an id, which no proposition can name. A theory
whose propositions relate no two constructs therefore comes back with no
vertices and no error.
}
\section{Measurement}{

The statements concern constructs, and a study measures them with error.
Error in a conditioning construct leaves part of the dependence that holding
it fixed should remove. A conditional statement tested on observed scores is
therefore rejected too often, and more often the larger the sample (Westfall
& Yarkoni, 2016). Take a chain whose two paths have standardised
coefficients of .5, with the middle construct measured at a reliability of
.8. A partial-correlation test at the 5 per cent level then rejects the true
statement in about 14, 29 and 51 per cent of studies of 200, 500 and 1,000
observations. Conditional statements are better tested with latent-variable
models, which take the measurement error into account (Westfall & Yarkoni,
2016). The measurement model that \code{\link[=tf_compile_sem]{tf_compile_sem()}} writes is one starting
point, and each statement can be tested in it separately (Thoemmes et al.,
2018).
}

\section{Feedback loops}{

With \code{cycles = "sigma"}, a graph whose directed edges form a cycle is
read by sigma-separation (Bongers et al., 2021), the criterion that holds
when each feedback loop, a strongly connected component of the directed
edges, has a unique equilibrium. The graph is replaced by its
acyclification (Definition A.13): every member of a loop takes the parents
of the whole loop from outside it, and the members of a loop are joined to
each other by bidirected edges. Each pair the theory leaves unjoined is then
tested in that graph as above, where m-separation equals sigma-separation in
the theory's own graph (Proposition A.19). There, two members of one loop
are always joined. So is a construct outside a loop to every member, when it
enters one of them or when it or a member of its own loop is associated with
one of them. No set separates such a pair, and when the theory itself leaves
the pair unjoined, it is listed under \code{inseparable}. A theory that
posits alternative stable states, such as a bistable network, has more than
one equilibrium and violates the assumption, and its sigma statements are
not guaranteed. On an acyclic graph, the two options give the same
statements. Applying d-separation to a cyclic graph, as dagitty does with
the \code{causal_dag} export, is valid only in special cases such as a linear
model.
}

\section{Refusals}{

The function stops in four cases. A value of \code{cycles} other than
\code{"refuse"} or \code{"sigma"}, or of \code{given} other than
\code{"basis"} or \code{"minimal"}, is refused before the theory is read. Two
constructs sharing an id would give one node two sets of parents, and a
proposition, directed or an association, that names an undeclared construct
would shrink the graph and so imply independencies the theory never claimed.
With \code{cycles = "refuse"}, the default, a cycle among the directed edges
is refused too, and the message names a cycle that was found and the
\code{"sigma"} option. Each refusal is an error of class \code{tf_refusal},
as are those of \code{\link[=tf_entailments]{tf_entailments()}}, \code{\link[=tf_equivalent_models]{tf_equivalent_models()}} and
\code{\link[=tf_discriminate]{tf_discriminate()}}, so that a caller can catch a refusal and nothing else.
}

\examples{
# A mediated chain commits the theory to one thing it does not state
# directly: arousal and avoidance are independent once threat is held fixed.
theory <- tf_theory("mediation", "A mediated chain") |>
  tf_add_construct("c_arousal", "Arousal", "Bodily activation.") |>
  tf_add_construct("c_threat", "Perceived threat", "Appraised danger.") |>
  tf_add_construct("c_avoidance", "Avoidance", "Withdrawal from the trigger.") |>
  tf_add_proposition("p1", "c_arousal", "c_threat", "increases") |>
  tf_add_proposition("p2", "c_threat", "c_avoidance", "increases")

implied <- tf_implications(theory)
implied$n_implications
implied$implications[[1]]$statement

# Stating that arousal and avoidance also covary for reasons the theory
# leaves open withdraws that claim: the pair is joined by an edge.
covary <- tf_add_proposition(theory, "p3", "c_arousal", "c_avoidance", "associates")
tf_implications(covary)$n_implications

# Closing a feedback loop from threat back to arousal makes the graph cyclic.
# The default refuses it, and sigma-separation keeps the claim, since holding
# threat fixed still cuts the one way out of the loop towards avoidance.
loop <- tf_add_proposition(theory, "p3", "c_threat", "c_arousal", "causes")
try(tf_implications(loop))
implied <- tf_implications(loop, cycles = "sigma")
lapply(implied$feedback, unlist)
implied$implications[[1]]$statement
}
